 "content": {
  "intro": "",
  "sections": [],
  "faq": [
   {
    "q": "How do I calculate the electrical load of my house?",
    "a": "List every appliance that can run at the same time, take the running watts from its label or the appliance list, multiply by how many you have and add them up. Divide the total by volts × power factor (230 V × 0.8 in Pakistan) for amps."
   },
   {
    "q": "What is the total load of a house in kW?",
    "a": "Most Pakistani homes without an AC run at well under one kilowatt; a home with one inverter AC, fans, lights and a fridge runs at about 3.1 kW, and each extra inverter AC adds about 1 kW on average and more at full speed."
   },
   {
    "q": "What is a connected load and how is it different from running load?",
    "a": "Connected load is the sum of everything wired in the house; running load is what is on at the same moment. Bills and sanctioned loads are set against expected demand, so the running load is the number to plan with."
   },
   {
    "q": "How many units does this load use per month?",
    "a": "Each appliance's watts × hours a day ÷ 1,000 gives its units a day; add them and multiply by 30. The default home above uses about 15.1 units a day and 454 a month."
   },
   {
    "q": "Can I use this load to size a UPS, inverter or solar system?",
    "a": "Yes. The running watts are the continuous rating your inverter or UPS needs (plus a safety margin), the peak watts are its surge rating, and the daily units are what a solar system must produce. The next-step links below take your numbers to those calculators."
   },
   {
    "q": "What is the electrical load estimation for a new house?",
    "a": "Add the rooms' lights and fans, one AC per bedroom you plan to cool, the kitchen appliances and the water pump, then apply a diversity factor because not everything runs together; the sanctioned-load calculator does this and recommends the kW to apply for."
   }
  ],
  "key_facts": [
   "Home electrical load in watts = the sum of the running watts of every appliance that is on at the same time; divide by volts × power factor to get amps.",
   "Peak load adds the starting surge of the largest motor or compressor only once, because two motors rarely start in the same instant.",
   "Monthly units (kWh) = watts × hours a day ÷ 1,000 × 30 days, added over every appliance; this is the number your electricity bill is based on.",
   "The default example home above (18 appliances of 6 kinds) draws 3,085 W running, 4,885 W at peak and uses about 454 units a month."
  ],
  "mistakes": [
   "Adding every appliance in the house. Only count what runs at the same time; an iron used for half an hour matters for the monthly units but rarely for the peak load.",
   "Using the AC's rated input as its average draw. An inverter AC's input varies with the room temperature; the watts here are a planning average, and the rated maximum is used for wire and breaker sizing.",
   "Ignoring starting surge. A non-inverter AC or a water pump draws about three times its running watts for a second or two, which is what trips an undersized breaker or inverter.",
   "Confusing watts with units. Watts are the rate of use right now; units (kWh) are watts × hours ÷ 1,000 and are what the bill charges for."
  ],
  "sources": [
   {
